In article <1992Mar2.022548.12446@cco.caltech.edu> wen-king@cs.caltech.edu (Wen-King Su) writes:
>From the look of the code, it appears that the bone files will be different
>not only for different machines, but also for different sets compilation
>parameters.
You may expect that most of the people uses all the options. If the byteorder
is the same only the 'scroll of mail' and BITFIELDs causes real trouble. The
bug of saving global information about objects does probably only affect
savefiles and not bonesfiles. A different C-compiler should not change the
format in many cases, because the C-compiler is not allowed to reorder a
struct.

When 3.1 is available I will look in the code and try to make some tools to
make it easy to save structs in a standarized format.

I already succeeded in sending the bonesfile to a specific address. I used
the following rules:

When the game saves a bonesfile it opens a little file. This file contains
the address of the mailserver, an idnr, a password and the probability of
exchanging. When nethack decides to exchange (using the probability in the
file) it uuencodes the bonesfile and sends it to the server with 'sendmail'.
The bonesfile will not be deleted.

The server is a program that will be run via a maildelivery. It uudecodes
the mail and saves it. It chooses a bones with the same level and with a
different idnr (if available) and sends it back.

The nethackclient also has a maildelivery. The alien bones will only be placed
when there exists a local bones on the level. The local bones will be deleted.

In this way nethackplayers will never get more or less bones than before. Also
losses of mail will have no effect on the number of boneslevels.

Perhaps this will work when everybody uses a Sparcstation and 'scroll of mail'.
I will continue my work when 3.1 is ready.

